<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osterode am Harz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osterode am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> is a town on the western edge of the Harz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. First recorded in the 12th century, it belonged to the Guelph Principality of Grubenhagen and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, and grew as a market and trading town serving the Harz <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines. Its large granary, the Kornmagazin of 1719–1722, stored grain for the miners.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osterode-am-harz.jpg" alt="Osterode am Harz" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otopon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altes_Rathaus_Osterode.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Osterode am <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, often simply called Osterode, is a town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> on the south-western edge of the Harz mountains. It was the seat of government of the district of Osterode and lies on the German Timber-Frame Road. The Söse River flows through the town from the reservoir of the Söse Dam, about 5 kilometres upstream; the dam, built in 1931, holds 25.5 million cubic metres and its water supplies drinking water as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Osterode was granted town rights in the early 13th century. Defensive walls were built in the 13th century and extended by 1330. In 1510 riots broke out and the townspeople murdered the mayor; Duke Philip I held a court session, the leaders of the riots were sentenced, town property was confiscated and municipal privileges were revoked. A fire struck the town in 1545, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> of 1618-1648 marching troops, marauders, looting, high war taxes and epidemics devastated it. Since 1665 it has belonged to the domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hanover">House of Hanover</a>, which also ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>.</p><p>In 1807 it became part of the Napoleonic Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, in 1813 it passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, in 1866 it was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and from 1871 it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. Its population was 7,467 in 1905. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> collapsed numerous local businesses, and in 1933 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> gained a majority in the town. From 1939 to 1941 the Oflag XI-A prisoner-of-war camp for Polish officers stood there, and from 1944 to 1945 there was a subcamp of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buchenwald-concentration-camp">Buchenwald concentration camp</a>. American troops captured Osterode in April 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town granary, built between 1719 and 1722 to supply the mining villages of the Upper Harz with grain, is among its most imposing buildings. After a comprehensive renovation it serves as the town hall, and a bas-relief of the 18th-century coat of arms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> decorates it. The Museum im Ritterhaus shows documents of the town's history from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the Lichtenstein Cave near Dorste, an archaeological site, yielded the bones of a man dated to about 1000 BCE. Osterode is the starting point of the 100-kilometre Harzer Hexenstieg, a hiking trail to Thale. The sculptor Tilman Riemenschneider was brought up locally.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osterode am Harz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the sons of Albert I of Brunswick divided their inheritance around 1291, Henry I the Admirable received Grubenhagen, named after a castle near Einbeck, with lands in the western Harz around <b>Osterode</b> and Herzberg.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-grubenhagen">Principality of Grubenhagen</a></li></ul></section>
